# mm.mcu

A portable microcontroller hardware interface, in partitions by facility. It is
pure C++: no foreign header, no C linkage, and no implementation of any facility.

The board partition describes the selected board at runtime. Board exposes the
complete ordered GPIO inventory as a read-only span, each entry carrying its
hardware number and human-readable name, and an optional LED attached to one of
those GPIO numbers with its active level. Provider-owned static storage makes
that vector-like view allocation-free. Future board-specific sensors and devices
extend this description as typed inventories; they do not change how the
platform provider is selected or how an application finds the board.

SPI configuration and span transfers are portable MCU facilities. Device
drivers own chip select and any data/command, reset, or busy GPIOs; the SPI
facility never changes those pins behind a driver's back.

I2C is the same kind of facility with a different selection mechanism: the
address selects the device, so every operation carries one and the configuration
does not. Addresses are seven-bit. i2c_write_read is one operation rather than a
write followed by a read, because a register read must not release the bus
between the two halves.

A platform supplies an object rather than functions. A name declared in this
module's purview is attached to it and cannot be defined from outside, so the seam
is a Platform subclass the platform's own module registers when it is linked in.
Whatever C the platform needs lives on its side of that seam.

platform-interface is the marker that makes the selected platform's provider part
of an executable's link closure, and only of the executables whose closure reaches
this module. The marker takes no value: module: above already names the interface,
and a second spelling of one name is a mismatch waiting to be diagnosed. Compiling,
checking, modelling, and documenting this module need no platform at all; a
provider becomes a requirement when an executable that reaches it is linked.

Partitions are listed before the primary interface because source order is
declared, and the primary re-exports them.
